<DocLayout
  title="MCP"
  description="Servers over stdio, HTTP, and SSE, with tool discovery and permissions in Sarthi Studio."
>
  <p>
    The <strong>Model Context Protocol (MCP)</strong> lets Studio connect to
    servers that expose tools the assistant can call. Tools are discovered
    automatically, validated against their schemas, and gated by permissions
    before the model can call them.
  </p>

  <h2>Transports</h2>
  <p>
    Studio connects to MCP servers over <strong>stdio</strong>, <strong>HTTP</strong>,
    and <strong>SSE</strong>. Each transport is a first-class option: stdio
    for local servers, HTTP and SSE for remote ones. The Connections view
    shows live status for every server.
  </p>

  <h2>Tool permissions</h2>
  <p>
    Tools are permission-gated before the model can call them. A tool
    permission rule decides whether a model may invoke a specific tool, and
    the agent still requires an approval rule to act on the tool's result.
    The tools that are connected and authorized are listed in the run so you
    can see exactly what the agent tried to call.
  </p>

  <h2>Trust boundary</h2>
  <p>
    Remote MCP servers are trusted by configuration, not by default. A
    server you add to a workspace is a server you have reviewed; Studio does
    not silently connect to arbitrary external servers.
  </p>

  <h2>Honest limits</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>The agent commands are permission-gated and auditable, but still run with your user privileges.</li>
    <li>Remote MCP servers are a potential data boundary — every tool result leaves the machine unless the server is local.</li>
  </ul>
</DocLayout>
